  "the population is E542's rows only; if E541 left the wrong surviving set, this rule selects from the wrong set and cannot tell",
  "one kept survivor is called decided, but the rule says nothing about whether that survivor is the node the census meant -- it only says the published line stops being ambiguous",
  "a row the rule would empty is published as refused rather than applied: silently dropping every survivor would delete the ambiguity instead of resolving it",
  "E540's rule is recomputed through E541's implementation on the survivors of the row rather than re-implemented here, so agreement means what E541 would say, not what a reimplementation might",
  "agreement compares the candidate's line and column; E540 publishes the located span, so two different nodes cannot be distinguished beyond that pair",
  "statement_line is E542's AST reading at the face's cutoff; a file edited since then shifts the statement and the row is published with an unresolved context rather than silently re-located",
  "a row whose survivor list is empty is classed as refused: there is nothing to anchor on, and this study does not manufacture a decision out of an empty set",
  "the rule uses one row-level field the census already publishes; it is not evidence that the census always publishes a line that opens a statement"
 ],
